  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A cold front will push across southeast Georgia \nand southeast South Carolina today, bringing a line of showers \nacross the region as well as gusty winds. \n\nEarly this morning, surface analysis shows the main front sits \njust to our west and runs north/south across central GA. There \nis good model agreement that the front will steadily push \neastward this morning, entering our interior GA counties around \n4 am then exiting east of Charleston by around 12-1 pm. In the \npre-frontal environment, precipitable water values are quite \nhigh and will continue to run in excess of 2.25 inches. Forcing \nalong the front and in the low-level jetting ahead of it will be\nsufficient to drive a band of passing showers with the front. \nIn the pre-dawn hours, there will be some embedded elements of \nmoderate to even heavy rainfall but shower motion should be fast\nenough to keep the flooding threat quite low. Also, there \nshouldn't be sufficient instability for anything other than \nshowers. \n\nAt sunrise, the front and associated band of showers is progged\nto be near the Savannah area and the along the Savannah River. \nThis would put the band of showers into the Charleston area \naround 10-11 am and exiting east after noon. Based on this \ntiming, there likely won't be enough time to allow instability \nto get too high, but there could still be 500-1,000 J/kg. So, \nthere could be a few rumbles of thunder. Model soundings also \nshow a decent uni-directional wind field, with 30-40 knots \ncentered around 850-900 mb. Therefore, these showers and \nembedded thunderstorms could be capable of producing some gusty \nwinds but will likely remain sub-severe. Behind the front, winds\nwill turn west-southwest and could produce some gusts into the \n25-30 mph range through the afternoon. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Minor coastal flooding is expected this morning \nacross coastal portions of Charleston and Colleton counties. \n\nMinor coastal flooding is expected to occur with the upcoming \nhigh tide (~9:15 am) across coastal Colleton and Charleston \ncounties. The astronomical high tide is 6.56 ft MLLW and as we \napproach low tide (~3 am) the tidal departure is running over 1 \nft. Winds will become less supportive of large tidal departures \nas they turn more southerly and eventually southwesterly over \nthe next few hours. But, we should still see enough departure to\nproduce minor coastal flooding and a Coastal Flood Advisory is \nin effect. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: There is a High Risk for rip currents along the \nCharleston County beaches today.\n\nThe combination of lingering elevated swell combined with \nbreezy southwesterly flow will support a High Risk for rip \ncurrents through the evening along the Charleston County \nbeaches. Otherwise, there is a Moderate Risk of rip currents for\nall other beaches.",
